        `The psychological impact of pornography is not identical for men and women, for singles and the married. Understanding the differences matters. For the single man, pornography constructs a fantasy template that real relationships cannot match. It trains the brain to associate sexual arousal with novelty, variety, passivity, and the complete absence of relational cost. Real intimacy — which is slow, vulnerable, reciprocal, and requires genuine knowledge of another person — becomes less neurologically stimulating than the screen. The man who arrives at marriage having consumed pornography heavily has brought with him a conditioned brain that is poorly suited for the exclusive, consistent, emotionally engaged intimacy that marriage requires. He is not merely carrying a bad habit. He is carrying a rewired reward system. For the single woman who consumes pornography — and the number is larger than the church acknowledges — the primary psychological damage is different. It is the damage of comparison and body dysmorphia. The bodies on screen are not representative. They are selected, augmented, lit, and edited. But the brain does not always process that analytically. The gap between the screen and her own body becomes a source of sustained, specific shame about her own appearance, desirability, and worth. She also experiences what researchers describe as emotional confusion — a difficulty distinguishing between genuine desire and conditioned response, between what she actually wants and what she has been trained to expect. For the married man, the research is consistent and sobering. Pornography use correlates directly with decreased marital satisfaction, reduced emotional intimacy with the spouse, and increased rates of infidelity. The mechanism is straightforward: the married man who uses pornography is not supplementing his marriage. He is constructing a parallel world that competes with it — and the marriage almost always loses. Gradually, then suddenly. For the married woman who discovers her husband's pornography use, the experience is psychologically identical to discovering a physical affair. The literature documents this clearly. She asks the same questions. She experiences the same betrayal, the same grief, the same corrosive self-questioning: am I not enough? What does she have that I do not? Why did he choose that over me? The theological answers to those questions, however true, do not resolve the psychological wound quickly or easily. She is not overreacting. She is responding accurately to a real violation of the covenant she believed she was in.`
